they're this, they're the same value.

00:14:43.565 --> 00:14:45.824
We don't know what the value
is, but you know that they're

00:14:45.825 --> 00:14:47.794
the same because it's constant.

00:14:48.694 --> 00:14:54.135
So now this formula here isn't
quite usable the way it is.

00:14:54.615 --> 00:14:59.255
What we can do is to make it
usable is take the reciprocal

00:14:59.454 --> 00:15:04.374
or the inverse, meaning flip the
fraction to put it in a usable form.

00:15:04.724 --> 00:15:06.554
So what does that flip fraction look like?

00:15:06.554 --> 00:15:13.204
Well, simply get rid of the one over TT
two and RT one and one and T two and two.

00:15:13.705 --> 00:15:16.125
We have our new formula,
which is just this.

00:15:16.625 --> 00:15:18.055
We got rid of everything else.

00:15:18.444 --> 00:15:21.725
You can do this with any
problem involving change.

00:15:21.725 --> 00:15:23.284
You can get rid of the constants.

00:15:24.864 --> 00:15:27.055
From that, what do we want to solve for?

00:15:27.064 --> 00:15:32.494
Well, we want N2 by itself, so
we'll divide both sides by T2,

00:15:33.305 --> 00:15:38.990
effectively moving it over, and
therefore getting our new formula.

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:41.580
So let's plug in our values.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:48.290
So T1 is 295 Kelvin and we are
going to be multiplying that by 0.

00:15:50.239 --> 00:15:51.530
27 moles.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:58.410
And then we're going to
divide that by 373 Kelvin.

00:15:59.319 --> 00:16:03.990
So now when that happens, what
you're going to get is 295 times 0.

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:09.959
27, divide that number
by 373 and we will get 0.

00:16:09.959 --> 00:16:11.099
21 moles.

00:16:11.544 --> 00:16:14.555
of air is left in your jar.

00:16:15.155 --> 00:16:17.834
So we can make a new formula.

00:16:17.844 --> 00:16:20.925
And one thing to keep in mind too,
is if you remember that relationship

00:16:20.925 --> 00:16:24.435
here, being there in an inverse
relationship, as temperature

00:16:24.444 --> 00:16:26.454
increases, moles should go down.

00:16:26.734 --> 00:16:29.024
And it did, which is great to see.

00:16:29.875 --> 00:16:33.505
Let's take a look at some different
skills to master in this video.

00:16:33.565 --> 00:16:38.315
The next skill that's going to be
very useful is how to find molar mass.

00:16:38.615 --> 00:16:40.705
Now, why is this something
that's so important?

00:16:40.715 --> 00:16:45.774
Well, it helps you to be able to
identify what gas you have in that

00:16:45.775 --> 00:16:48.025
you can't usually see the gases.

00:16:48.375 --> 00:16:51.895
So you can measure what they're all
about, but you might not be able to just

00:16:51.895 --> 00:16:55.135
identify by looking around you being
like, Oh, what gas is all around my head.

00:16:55.535 --> 00:16:58.075
You have to be able to do
calculations to solve that.

00:16:58.655 --> 00:17:01.225
So in this problem, I have 2.

00:17:01.265 --> 00:17:07.734
787 grams sample of gas, and it's
found to occupy a volume of 2.

00:17:07.735 --> 00:17:08.285
45 liters.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.829
At 300 Kelvin and 1 atmosphere.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:14.920
We know that it's an elemental diatomic.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:16.350
Which one is it?

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:17.530
Let's try to find out.

00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:20.849
So the first thing to think of is,
well, when it comes to molar mass,

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:25.370
which allows you to identify different
substances, it's like the fingerprint

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.800
for a lot of these molecules.

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:28.690
It's one way to do that.

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:31.460
So if I am to think of molar mass, right?

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.580
Like that was grams over.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:39.800
Moles, well in this problem
I have my grams, right?

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:45.590
So the way that we could
essentially solve this is I know 2.

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:45.609
787 moles.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:51.800
grams on top, but I don't
know my moles, which is N.

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:54.910
So which formula can solve for moles?

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:57.599
Well, this is where we'll
use the ideal gas law.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.350
PV is equal to nRT.

00:18:00.289 --> 00:18:03.959
So being that is one thing you
will notice is that this particular

00:18:03.959 --> 00:18:06.430
gas is at its current condition.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:09.090
Notice how there's no change involved.

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:12.960
There's one volume, one
pressure, one temperature.

00:18:13.209 --> 00:18:14.500
There's nothing else.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:18.030
So what we are going to end up
doing is we are going to plug

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:20.410
those values and solve for N.

00:18:20.969 --> 00:18:26.889
So we are going to rearrange the ideal gas
law to get N by itself, and it's going to

00:18:26.889 --> 00:18:31.829
be pressure times volume divided by R T.

00:18:32.779 --> 00:18:34.910
So now what is R in case you're wondering?

00:18:34.939 --> 00:18:36.969
That's just a constant value.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:41.590
And we're going to use it that we got
to always make sure the units match

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:43.800
what we are being given in the problem.

00:18:44.429 --> 00:18:46.239
So if they don't match, you're
going to get, you're going to

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.520
be really sad because you're not
going to get the correct answer.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:52.929
So our pressure is one atmosphere,
so that's going to go on top.

00:18:54.009 --> 00:18:56.659
We have our volume, which is 2.

00:18:56.719 --> 00:19:04.810
45 liters, and that's going to
be divided by R, which is 0.

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:05.129
08206.

00:19:07.135 --> 00:19:11.905
And that has a very complex set of
units, liter, atmosphere, mole, kelvin.

00:19:12.675 --> 00:19:17.334
And then that's going to be times
temperature, which is 300 kelvin.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:22.470
Okay, now when we're putting this in
our calculators, you need to make sure

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:26.960
that you are either using the fraction
button to separate top and bottom or use

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.210
parentheses to separate them as well.

00:19:30.069 --> 00:19:34.010
So when we solve this, what we are
going to get for the number of moles

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:37.579
in the problem is a number around 0.

00:19:37.579 --> 00:19:41.570
0995 and then a bunch of
decimals after that, and that's

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:43.740
going to be units of moles.

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:46.399
Now, does that match any molar mass?

00:19:46.459 --> 00:19:47.899
Well, we're not quite done, right?

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:53.539
This number here has to, you
have to plug it in to the value,

00:19:53.629 --> 00:19:55.729
to the term that we had above.

00:19:56.315 --> 00:20:02.155
So if we take this value from
before, so we are going to

00:20:02.385 --> 00:20:05.255
plug in our new number for n.

00:20:06.375 --> 00:20:09.475
So let's take that and this
will be grams over moles.

00:20:10.655 --> 00:20:13.944
And what, by putting these two
things in your calculator, you're

00:20:13.945 --> 00:20:15.710
essentially Simplifying it.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:17.190
So we do 2.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:23.900
787 divide by 0995, hit enter,
and what you are going to get is a

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:29.020
value close to 28 grams per mole.

00:20:29.639 --> 00:20:31.669
Now which diatomic gas is this?

00:20:31.939 --> 00:20:32.350
Is it?

00:20:32.459 --> 00:20:36.529
Well, that's one thing you have to be
able to remember, is your diatomics.

00:20:36.929 --> 00:20:40.469
And if you're not sure about them,
watch my video on the seven diatomic

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:42.229
gases, because it's very important.

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:44.930
So let's take a look at
the list of diatomics.

00:20:45.009 --> 00:20:49.430
Alright, if you remember my saying,
have no fear of ice cold beer, you

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:51.900
will never forget the seven diatomics.

00:20:52.389 --> 00:20:54.340
So diatomic means two.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:21:00.405
So being that we had a molar mass of 28
grams per mole, we're So if you divide

00:21:00.435 --> 00:21:02.655
that number by 2, you will get 14.

00:21:03.615 --> 00:21:06.235
So which element matches 14?

00:21:06.655 --> 00:21:07.844
Well, it's not hydrogen.

00:21:07.935 --> 00:21:09.764
Oh, nitrogen is 14.

00:21:10.114 --> 00:21:12.405
So 14 times 2 is 28.

00:21:12.875 --> 00:21:18.314
So our diatomic in this case is N2 gas.

00:21:18.825 --> 00:21:21.635
And that will be our
answer for this problem.

00:21:22.165 --> 00:21:25.365
So sometimes you got to try to
solve the mystery of what gas

00:21:25.385 --> 00:21:26.935
can be located in our sample.

00:21:27.274 --> 00:21:28.475
So we did it this time.

00:21:28.475 --> 00:21:30.065
Let's see if we can do
it again in the future.

00:21:30.585 --> 00:21:32.725
All right, let's go on
to the next problem.

00:21:32.804 --> 00:21:33.294
Okay.

00:21:33.295 --> 00:21:37.064
Sometimes you will be given problems
that involve diagrams and you have

00:21:37.065 --> 00:21:40.325
to be able to read what they're
showing, especially when they show

00:21:40.325 --> 00:21:42.835
them in different atoms and molecules.

00:21:43.235 --> 00:21:46.065
And a lot of times they're
referring to mixtures.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:49.910
So in this case, we have a mixture and
it's sealed in this container that I have.

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:53.110
Let's use a diagram to answer
the following questions.

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:56.750
So the first thing is you can see
that in this purple vial here,

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.240
I have four different gases.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.390
And the first thing I ask
you is, okay, which gas has

00:22:01.399 --> 00:22:03.520
the highest partial pressure?

00:22:03.809 --> 00:22:05.159
Well, how can we do that?

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:08.940
Well, this is where Dalton's
law comes into play.

00:22:09.504 --> 00:22:12.695
Dalton's law had two different formulas
that were really important, right?

00:22:12.705 --> 00:22:16.845
If you add up every pressure of each
gas, you will get the total pressure.

00:22:17.534 --> 00:22:18.484
Same thing about moles.

00:22:18.485 --> 00:22:21.664
If you add the moles of each
individual gas, being that they're

00:22:21.735 --> 00:22:26.354
indistinguishable particles from one
another, you can't just count all the

00:22:26.354 --> 00:22:29.494
little air molecules around you and
figure out what's nitrogen, oxygen.

00:22:29.824 --> 00:22:31.844
You just know what the
pressure of your room is.

00:22:31.864 --> 00:22:34.894
You don't know what's each,
but we can try to calculate it.

00:22:34.964 --> 00:22:35.824
We can approximate.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:42.520
So based on that, what that means is,
well, whichever molecule has the most

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:45.969
moles is exuding your highest pressure.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.629
So what we need to simply do
in these types of problems

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:52.040
is count what we have, right?

00:22:52.079 --> 00:22:53.420
As if you could count.

00:22:53.889 --> 00:22:57.510
So if we count the number of oxygens
here, we're going to count each

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.230
individual one of these little orbs here.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:04.390
So you see a one, two, three,
four, five, six, seven red oxygens.

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:06.310
So we are going to put a.

00:23:08.190 --> 00:23:13.260
For nitrogen or ammonia, we have
one, two, three, four, five.

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:17.200
We will put a five here for HCL gas.

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:22.209
We have one, two, three,
four, five as well.

00:23:22.889 --> 00:23:30.019
And then neon, which is our purple,
we have one, two, three, four in our

00:23:30.539 --> 00:23:35.740
molecule here in our different, uh,
our number of Forbes that we have.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:36.409
All right.

00:23:36.409 --> 00:23:38.560
So which one is the highest?

00:23:38.929 --> 00:23:43.470
Well, being that oxygen has the
most, this is, this is having the

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:45.669
most pressure out of all of them.

00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:52.600
So oxygen will be the most, the least
amount of pressure would be neon because

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:54.850
it has the lowest number of moles.

00:23:55.430 --> 00:23:57.690
So O2 is our answer.

00:23:58.309 --> 00:23:59.410
Now let's go on to another one.

00:24:00.139 --> 00:24:06.730
So if we assume each gas particle is
one mole, what is the mole fraction NH3?

00:24:07.515 --> 00:24:11.165
Well, okay, so mole fraction,
what is that referring to?

00:24:11.615 --> 00:24:17.245
Well, if you look at this formula here,
where partial pressure is equal to the

00:24:17.245 --> 00:24:24.625
total pressure times n one, n I over n
total, what this part is in parentheses

00:24:24.635 --> 00:24:28.805
here, this is your mole fraction.

00:24:29.085 --> 00:24:29.295
Okay.

00:24:29.295 --> 00:24:31.725
So mole fraction.

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:39.560
And usually the way that this guy is,
is labeled is with the Greek letter I to

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:42.620
represent that you have a mole fraction.

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:47.110
So if you ever see that, like that fancy
looking X, that's telling you, Hey,

00:24:47.179 --> 00:24:49.200
this is talking about the mole fraction.

00:24:49.819 --> 00:24:53.030
So the way it is, is if you remember
before I counted everything for us.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.360
So if we add up all those,
remember each one is equal to one.

00:24:57.850 --> 00:25:00.190
So we can get our total really easily.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:03.560
So we do 7 plus 5, that's 12.

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:05.409
12 plus 5 is 17.

00:25:05.409 --> 00:25:07.390
17 plus 4 is 21.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:13.129
So what we will put as our total, so
we'll put that on the bottom, so we got

00:25:13.149 --> 00:25:20.580
21 moles total of gas, and that's adding
all the and it's asking us what's the,

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:23.370
what's the mole fraction of ammonia.

00:25:23.955 --> 00:25:28.225
So ammonia we had five from
earlier, so we are going to put

00:25:28.225 --> 00:25:31.495
that we had five moles of ammonia.

00:25:32.125 --> 00:25:35.925
So this is the literal
definition of a mole fraction,

00:25:35.925 --> 00:25:37.785
so five over 21 in this case.

00:25:38.385 --> 00:25:41.204
Now depending on who your teacher
is and how the homeworks are, you

00:25:41.204 --> 00:25:45.165
either have to put it as a fraction,
so five over 21, or you can put that

00:25:45.165 --> 00:25:47.165
in your calculator and simplify it.

00:25:47.535 --> 00:25:52.150
So if we did five divided
by 21, we will get 0.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:52.930
238.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:57.210
With that value, you will notice
that there is no units between it

00:25:57.230 --> 00:26:00.980
being that moles technically cancels
here because it's just a fraction.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:04.009
It's just a ratio of what
we're trying to represent.

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:09.200
Now the last part is assume each gas
represents one mole like we did before.

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:12.630
The total pressure of the container is 1.

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:14.250
20 atmospheres.

00:26:14.825 --> 00:26:18.645
What is the partial
pressure, pressure of HCL?

00:26:19.274 --> 00:26:24.455
So earlier I had that five mole, there
was five moles of HCL just to remind

00:26:24.455 --> 00:26:26.694
us of how many we had in our container.

00:26:27.404 --> 00:26:29.135
So how can we do this?

00:26:29.145 --> 00:26:33.004
So let's use this partial pressure
equation that we've been, I've

00:26:33.004 --> 00:26:34.664
been kind of dancing around.

00:26:35.134 --> 00:26:38.924
So in this case, we want to know
what the partial pressure of HCL

00:26:39.384 --> 00:26:41.054
is so that we'll replace our I.

00:26:41.429 --> 00:26:44.090
Because I is just an indice,
it's just however you label it.

00:26:44.409 --> 00:26:46.899
In this case, we are labeling it as HCl.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:52.229
So we have our total pressure, which is 1.

00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:58.059
20 atmospheres, and then we're going
to times that by our mole fraction.

00:26:58.579 --> 00:27:05.500
So that was 5 moles all over 21,
which was the total number of moles.

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:08.620
So that was all the little molecules
that were in the container.

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:10.530
So when we do 1.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:16.120
2 times 5 divided by 21, what
we are going to get is 0.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:21.539
286 atmospheres of HCl.

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:25.910
And atm is atmospheres, not at
the moment, it is atmospheres,

00:27:26.270 --> 00:27:27.719
just so everybody knows.

00:27:28.219 --> 00:27:32.330
All right, so that is our
value here for this problem.

